{"name":"Shark Tooth","hex":"#e4e1d3","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"shark","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any predatory fish of the superorder Selachimorpha, with a cartilaginous skeleton and 5 to 7 gill slits on each side of its head.","Meat of this animal, consumed as food."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To fish for sharks.","Of a university student who is not a fresher (first-year undergraduate), and especially towards the start of the academic year:\n To engage in sexual activity with a fresher.\n To cruise for casual sex with a fresher at a bar or club."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shark"},{"term":"tooth","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard, calcareous structure present in the mouth of many vertebrate animals, generally used for biting and chewing food.","A sharp projection on the blade of a saw or similar implement."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To provide or furnish with teeth.","To indent; to jag."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tooth"}],"entries":[{"title":"Shark tooth","blocks":[{"text":"Sharks continually shed their teeth; some Carcharhiniformes shed approximately 35,000 teeth in a lifetime, replacing those that fall out. There are four basic types of shark teeth: dense flattened, needle-like, pointed lower with triangular upper, and non-functional. The type of tooth that a shark has depends on its diet and feeding habits."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/CretaceousSharkTeeth061812.JPG/330px-CretaceousSharkTeeth061812.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shark_tooth","wikidata":"Q626192"},{"title":"Shark","blocks":[{"text":"Sharks are a group of elasmobranch cartilaginous fishes characterized by a ribless endoskeleton, dermal denticles, five to seven gill slits on each side, and pectoral fins that are not fused to the head. Modern sharks are classified within the division Selachii and are the sister group to the Batomorphi. Some sources extend the term \"shark\" as an informal category including extinct members of Chondrichthyes with a shark-like morphology, such as hybodonts. Shark-like chondrichthyans such as Cladoselache and Doliodus first appeared in the Devonian Period, though some fossilized chondrichthyan-like scales are as old as the Late Ordovician. The earliest confirmed modern sharks (Selachii) are known from the Early Jurassic around 200 million years ago, with the oldest known member being Agaleus, though records of true sharks may extend back as far as the Permian."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Corl0207_%2828225976491%29.jpg/330px-Corl0207_%2828225976491%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shark","wikidata":"Q7372"},{"title":"Tooth","blocks":[{"text":"A tooth is a hard, calcified structure found in the jaws of many vertebrates and used to break down food. Some animals, particularly carnivores and omnivores, also use teeth to help with capturing or wounding prey, tearing food, for defensive purposes, to intimidate other animals often including their own, or to carry prey or their young. The roots of teeth are covered by gums. Teeth are not made of bone, but rather of multiple tissues of varying density and hardness that originate from the outermost embryonic germ layer, the ectoderm."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Close_up_-_chimpanzee_teeth.png/330px-Close_up_-_chimpanzee_teeth.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tooth","wikidata":"Q553"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Steensen_-_Elementorum_myologiae_specimen%2C_1669_-_4715289.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Steensen_-_Elementorum_myologiae_specimen%2C_1669_-_4715289.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Steensen_-_Elementorum_myologiae_specimen%2C_1669_-_4715289.tif/lossy-page1-1280px-Steensen_-_Elementorum_myologiae_specimen%2C_1669_-_4715289.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Steensen_-_Elementorum_myologiae_specimen%2C_1669_-_4715289.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Steensen_-_Elementorum_myologiae_specimen%2C_1669_-_4715289.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Elementorum myologiae specimen, 1669","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASteensen_-_Elementorum_myologiae_specimen%2C_1669_-_4715289.tif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Eastman1901-web.jpg/330px-Eastman1901-web.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Eastman1901-web.jpg/330px-Eastman1901-web.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Eastman1901-web","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEastman1901-web.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Carcharodon_megalodon_fossil_shark_jaw_%28reconstruction%29_%28late_Cenozoic%29_2.jpg/500px-Carcharodon_megalodon_fossil_shark_jaw_%28reconstruction%29_%28late_Cenozoic%29_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Carcharodon_megalodon_fossil_shark_jaw_%28reconstruction%29_%28late_Cenozoic%29_2.jpg/1280px-Carcharodon_megalodon_fossil_shark_jaw_%28reconstruction%29_%28late_Cenozoic%29_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Carcharodon_megalodon_fossil_shark_jaw_%28reconstruc